===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|haček}} (attested since 1956), {{l|en|hacek}} (1959), {{l|en|hachek}} (1969)
* {{qualifier|rare}} {{l|en|haċek}} (1967), {{l|en|hatcheck}} (1981), {{l|en|hatschek}} (1983), {{l|en|hatchek}} (1988), {{l|en|hacheck}} (1990), {{l|en|hac̬ek}} (1992), {{l|en|haczek}} (1995), {{l|en|hácek}} (1997), {{l|en|haĉek}} (2002), {{l|en|haceck}} (2003)

The central consonant, {{IPAchar|/tʃ/}}, is variously Anglicized as ''ch'' or ''tch'', [[Germanize]]d as ''tsch'', [[Polonize]]d as ''cz'', or left as ''c'', either bare or adorned with a {{l|en|tečka}} (''ċ''), {{l|en|circumflex}} (''ĉ'') or háček below it (''c̬''). The final consonant is sometimes written ''-ck'' instead of ''-k''.

[[File:Examples of háčky 2.svg|thumb|Háčeks adorning, from left to right:<br/>c, e, l, ü (used in pinyin) and [[σ]] (as in Cypriot Greek)]]

===Etymology===
First attested in 1951; from the {{bor|en|cs|háček||háček|lit=little hook}}, the diminutive of {{m|cs|hák||hook}} (from {{der|en|gmh|hāken}}, from {{der|en|goh|hāko||hook}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hakô||hook}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*keg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*keng-||peg, hook}}) + the diminutive suffix {{m|cs|-ek}}. Cognate with and formed like {{cog|en|hooklet}} and {{cog|de|Häkchen}}. Also cognate with {{cog|ang|haca||hook, door-fastening}} and modern {{cog|en|hake}} (more information below).

