{{col-top|2|a survey of other sources’ coverages}}
A survey of eleven other lexicographical sources reveals that ''[http://lookwayup.com/free/about.htm LookWAYup]'' and ''[http://www.vocabulary.com/ Vocabulary.com]'' fully define {{m|en||wedge}} in the relevant sense, whilst ''[[w:Reference.com|Dictionary.com]]'' and the ''[[w:Random House Webster's Unabridged Dictionary|Random House Unabridged Dictionary]]'' (1997) give the typographic-''cum''-orthographic sense the one-word gloss “haček”, and ''[[w:WordNet|WordNet 3.0]]'' codefines it with {{m|en||háček}}; the six other sources, namely the ''[[w:The American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language|American Heritage Dictionary]]'', the ''[[w:Collins English Dictionary|Collins English Dictionary]]'', the ''[[w:Compact Oxford English Dictionary|Compact Oxford English Dictionary]]'', ''[https://web.archive.org/web/20120211074604/http://www.dictionarist.com/en/about.asp Dictionarist.com]'', the ''[[w:Oxford English Dictionary|Oxford English Dictionary]]'' [2nd ed., 1989], and the ''[[w:Ultralingua|UltraLingua]] [http://www.ultralingua.com/onlinedictionary/ English Dictionary]'', all omit this sense of {{m|en||wedge}}. All eleven sources list and define {{m|en||háček}}. Not one of the sources lists an entry for {{m|en||čiriklo}}, {{m|en||clicka}}, {{m|en||inverted caret}}, {{m|en||inverted circumflex}}, {{m|en||inverted hat}}, {{m|en||mäkčeň}}, {{m|en||palatal hook}}, or {{m|en||strešica}}; neither does any of them include the relevant sense in any of their entries for {{m|en||caret}}, {{m|en||chevron}}, {{m|en||hat}}, {{m|en||hook}}, or {{m|en||wing}}.
